From 79327f103ee717ad679a6691d64ef2ae4c31ea10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 09:22:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assistant: reply like the shop, not like a form
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three separate causes, found by running the eval harness against the live
model rather than by reading the prompt.

1. Nearly half of all customers were answered by a canned template. The
   provider told the model its reply 'must match this JSON Schema exactly'
   and then printed the schema; on 10 of 22 eval messages an 8B model
   satisfied that by reproducing the schema. A decision that fails to parse
   falls through to template_reply, so 'Good morning', 'wetin you get?' and
   'I want 2 bags of rice 50kg' all reached the customer as a form letter
   while the logs recorded the assistant as having run. The instruction now
   distinguishes an instance from the schema it conforms to.

2. The system prompt asked for the flatness. 'Be brief. One or two sentences
   is usually right. No greetings unless they greeted you, no sign-off' is a
   description of a form letter. It now says who the model is — the person
   who answers this shop's WhatsApp — and asks for the shape a shopkeeper
   actually uses: answer the question that was asked, then the obvious next
   step. 'Do you have rice?' is a yes-or-no question and is now answered as
   one, rather than with a bare price.

3. Every shop sounded the same because every shop was given the same prompt
   and no voice. The model now sees up to six real replies the owner typed
   from their own phone (sentFromApp echoes, which cannot have been written
   by this assistant), labelled tone-only so a stale price in an old message
   is never reused as a fact.

Temperature moves off zero for the same reason: this call writes the
sentence a customer reads, and greedy decoding gives every customer the same
clause. The facts were never protected by determinism — they are protected
by applyServerInvariants and the grounding validator, which check the
figures in the finished text against the rows actually retrieved.

653 tests still pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
